# Implement handwriting roles and sizes

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical implementation notes describe domain-only evidence. The finite
acceptance below does not claim full sampling, rendering or quality testing.

Allow one profile to expose body, title, subtitle, label, caption, formula,
margin, and annotation roles without pretending they are unrelated writers.

Current executable evidence now centralizes those eight handwriting roles and
stores the profile identity once around caller-supplied role presentations. Role
entries therefore carry size/style metadata but cannot independently claim a
different writer identity. A read-only role lookup returns `None` for a missing
role and refuses an ambiguous duplicate claim instead of silently choosing one.

A complete read-only audit now walks the canonical eight-role order and reports
each role as Missing, Unique, or Ambiguous together with every caller-order
presentation index. It does not synthesize defaults or declare any global
validity policy. A 4,681-case exhaustive oracle covers every sequence through
four supplied presentations over all eight roles and cross-checks exact lookup,
duplicate indices, and complete audit evidence against an independent oracle.

Size units, role defaults, style vocabulary, global duplicate/missing-role
policy, glyph behavior, and semantic document-style mappings remain open.

## Finite single-writer role and two-size presentation contract

**Inputs:** one exact selected handwriting-profile identity, its eight-role
audit, role-specific calibrated dimensions and a requested semantic text role.
**Outputs:** one unambiguous role presentation at an admitted small/large
size, a visibly accepted fallback to another role of the *same* writer,
or `UnavailableRole`. No role may silently change profile identity.

**First-release roles:** `Body`, `Title`, `Subtitle`, `Label`, `Caption`,
`Formula`, `Margin` and `Annotation` belong to one profile. V1 activation
requires complete explicit size/metric claims for `Body`, `Title`, `Label`,
and `Annotation`; the other four remain separately exposed but may be missing
until supported. Each role has one unique profile entry containing its two
named size variants: variants must not be represented as duplicate role
entries, because ambiguous role lookup currently fails closed.

**Proposed finite metric matrix (illustrative, not calibrated):**

| Role | Small / large cap height | Baseline and width policy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Body | 3.0 / 4.0 mm | Stable body baseline; measured advance |
| Title | 5.0 / 7.0 mm | Title baseline; same writer strokes |
| Label | 2.5 / 3.5 mm | Compact baseline; same writer strokes |
| Annotation | 2.5 / 3.0 mm | Margin baseline; same writer strokes |

**Selection rule:** a `Body` span requesting large maps to the exact selected
profile's `Body/large` measured metrics and compatible contextual stroke
forms. Role changes may alter size, entry/exit rules and spacing but cannot
invent a different hand or reuse a contour beyond its calibrated envelope.
The four other declared roles remain in the eight-role audit and cannot be
implicitly mapped to Body or Title just because their entry is missing.

**Worked example:** selected profile `student-A` has one `Body` entry with
small=3.0 mm and large=4.0 mm. An accepted paragraph `p1` requests
`Body/large` and a title `t1` requests `Title/small=5.0 mm`; both retain
`student-A` in their derived provenance. If `Formula` is missing, a user may
explicitly choose a declared visible `Body/small` approximation only after
the backend confirms formula glyph coverage and records that choice.

**Refusal example:** duplicate `Title` entries, absent `Annotation/large`,
invalid measured cap height or `Formula` requiring unknown symbols refuses
role selection and blocks affected output until explicit repair/fallback.
A silently substituted system typeface or a second writer profile is not an
acceptable default. Scaling outside measured bounds cannot be labeled as
the same verified handwriting evidence.

**Assumptions and limits:** one profile-owned role lookup and eight-role
audit exist, but size units, variant storage, physical metrics and cross-role
fallback policy do not. The four numerical rows are theoretical examples,
not measured claims or implemented preview/rendering modes.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for the same-profile role inventory/audit; theoretical
  for two-size variant metrics and declared fallbacks, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** eight-role identity, four core roles with two sizes each,
  worked selection and missing/ambiguous refusal.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** metric calibration, size variants,
  style/role command transport or handwriting output.
